 ],
 "literal": "If he obtained a small amount of toddy, he would give it to us, alas! / If he obtained a large amount of toddy, / as we sang, he would rejoice and drink, alas! / Even with a small amount of food, he would share in many plates, alas! / Even with abundant food, he would share in many plates, alas! / Wherever there was meat with bone, he would give it to us, alas! / Wherever arrows and spears entered, he himself would stand, alas! / With his hand smelling of fragrant paste, / he would caress my head smelling of raw meat, alas! / Passing through the emptiness of the wide begging bowls of the great bards with precious heads, / making the pupils of the sorrowful eyes of the suppliants droop, / on the tongues of the poets of beautiful words and subtle wisdom, / it went and fell, his / spear that pierced his precious chest! / Where is my father, who was my support, oh? / Hereafter, there are no singers, and there are no givers of gifts to those who sing! / Like the great, fragrant pakaṉṟai flowers of the cold water-ghat / that wither without being worn, / there are very many lives that perish without giving anything to others!",
 "translation": "If he got a little toddy, he would give it to us. If he got a lot, he would drink it happily while we sang. Even with a little rice, he shared it in many bowls. Even with a feast of rice, he shared it in many bowls. Wherever there was meat on the bone, he gave it to us. Wherever arrows and spears pierced, he stood there himself. With his hand fragrant with lemon grass, he would caress my head that smelled of raw flesh. Passing through the empty bowls of the great bards, making the pupils of the weeping eyes of the beggars droop, and silencing the tongues of the poets of beautiful words and subtle wisdom, it went and fell—the spear that pierced his precious chest! Where is my father, my support, now? No more are there singers, and no more are there givers to those who sing. Like the great, fragrant pakaṉṟai flowers of the cold water-ghat that wither without being worn by anyone, there are so many lives that perish without giving anything to others!",
 "modern": "சிறிதளவு கள் கிடைத்தாலும் அதனை எமக்கே தருவான். மிகுதியாகக் கிடைத்தாலோ, யாம் பாடத் தான் மகிழ்ந்து குடிப்பான். சிறிய அளவிலான உணவாயினும் பல கலங்களில் பகிர்ந்து உண்பான். பெரிய அளவிலான உணவாயினும் பல கலங்களில் பகிர்ந்து உண்பான். எலும்புடன் கூடிய இறைச்சி உள்ள பகுதிகளை எல்லாம் எமக்கே தருவான். அம்புகளும் வேல்களும் பாயும் போர்க்களத்தில் தான் முன்னின்று தடுப்பான். நரந்தம் மணம் வீசும் தன் கையால், புலால் நாற்றம் வீசும் என் தலையை அன்போடு தடவிக் கொடுப்பான். சிறந்த பாணர்களின் அகன்ற இரப்பக் கலங்களின் வறுமையைப் போக்கி, இரப்போரின் துன்பக் கண்ணீர் வழிய, அழகிய சொற்களையும் நுட்பமான அறிவையும் உடைய புலவர்களின் நாவில் சென்று வீழ்ந்தது, அவனது அரிய மார்பில் பாய்ந்த வேல்! எமக்கு ஆதரவாக இருந்த என் தந்தை இப்போது எங்கு இருக்கிறானோ? இனிப் பாடுவாரும் இல்லை; பாடுவோர்க்குப் பரிசில் தருவாரும் இல்லை. குளிர்ந்த நீர்நிலையின் துறையிலுள்ள, நறுமணம் மிக்க பெரிய பகன்றை மலர்கள் எவராலும் சூடப்படாமல் வீணே வாடிப் போவதைப் போல, பிறருக்கு ஒன்றும் ஈயாமல் இறந்து போகும் உயிர்கள் உலகில் மிகப்பலவே!",
 "explanation": "This poem is a moving lament (kaiyaṟunilai) sung by the famous poet Avvaiyār on the death of her patron, the chieftain Atiyamāṉ Neṭumāṉ Añci. She recalls his immense generosity and intimacy, contrasting how he gave away even the smallest portions of food and drink to his poets, while taking the brunt of battle upon himself. The spear that pierced Atiyamāṉ's chest did not just kill him; it effectively pierced the begging bowls of the bards, brought tears to the eyes of the needy, and silenced the tongues of the wise poets. Avvaiyār laments that with his death, there is no one left to sing or to give. She compares the useless, ordinary people who live and die without giving anything to others to the beautiful pakaṉṟai flowers that grow on cold riverbanks and wither away without ever being worn by anyone.",
